---
/**
 * ReloadTopGuard: a refresh lands at the TOP, without a journey.
 *
 * Moved verbatim out of Hero.astro, which used to be its only mount. The
 * homepage no longer renders Hero (feat/homepage-v4), and with it went the
 * guard, so a refresh after scrolling painted the restored offset instead of
 * the top: scripts/e2e-reload-scroll.test.mjs caught it. One component, both
 * mounts, so the two cannot drift. Render it EARLY in the page: it is an
 * inline script precisely so it runs during parse, before the browser's own
 * restore can paint (see the long notes below, kept as they were).
 *
 * scripts/splash-scroll-lock.test.mjs pins its jumpToTop() flush and its
 * hold-the-top guard; scripts/e2e-reload-scroll.test.mjs measures both.
 */
---

<script is:inline>
    // Prevent the browser from automatically restoring the scroll position on refresh,
    // which breaks the initial scroll-linked animations and traps the user.
    if ('scrollRestoration' in history) {
      history.scrollRestoration = 'manual';
    }
    
    /*
      Reset the scroll position on a reload — INSTANTLY.

      `html { scroll-behavior: smooth }` is set globally in global-base.css, so
      a bare `window.scrollTo(0, 0)` does not jump, it ANIMATES. On a refresh
      part-way down the page that turns the reset into a visible glide from the
      restored offset up to the top, and because three resets fire in sequence
      (here, on the next frame, and on pageshow) they interrupt and re-target
      each other — then arm() jumps to 0 while an animation is still in flight,
      producing the jarring up/down bounce this block is supposed to prevent.

      This is the inline copy of `jumpTo()` in src/lib/scroll-to.ts — read the
      full rationale there, including why the forced recalc is load-bearing and
      why `behavior: 'instant'` is not used. It is duplicated rather than
      imported because an `is:inline` script never goes through the bundler,
      and this has to run during parse: by the time a module has loaded, the
      browser has already restored the offset and painted it.
      scripts/splash-scroll-lock.test.mjs keeps the two copies honest.
    */
    function jumpToTop() {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const previousBehavior = root.style.scrollBehavior;
      root.style.scrollBehavior = 'auto';
      void root.offsetHeight; // forces the recalc; without it scrollTo still sees `smooth`
      window.scrollTo(0, 0);
      root.style.scrollBehavior = previousBehavior;
    }

    const navEntries = performance.getEntriesByType ? performance.getEntriesByType('navigation') : [];

    /** @type {any} */
    const p = window.performance;
    const legacyNav = p ? p.navigation : null;
    const isReload = (navEntries.length > 0 && navEntries[0].type === 'reload') ||
                     (legacyNav && legacyNav.type === 1);

    if (isReload) {
      jumpToTop();
      requestAnimationFrame(jumpToTop);
      // Fallback for Safari which occasionally restores scroll after the first paint
      window.addEventListener('pageshow', jumpToTop, { once: true });

      /*
        ─── HOLD THE TOP UNTIL THE READER ASKS TO LEAVE IT ────────────────────

        The three resets above are one-shot: each corrects the offset at one
        instant. `history.scrollRestoration = 'manual'` is supposed to stop the
        engine restoring at all, but iOS Safari restores anyway, once, at a
        moment that moves with load timing — so a fixed number of shots can
        always be beaten by a restore that lands between them.

        Measured before this guard, walking the restore across the startup
        window (7 timings from DOMContentLoaded+0ms to +600ms, both
        orientations): EVERY timing painted 8–14 frames at the restored offset
        of 390px before something snapped it back, and one timing was never
        corrected at all and simply stayed there. In landscape those frames
        also carry the hero parallax at its 150px cap, because the parallax is
        only disabled on portrait touch — which is why landscape looks the
        worst and why the reader sees the page lurch DOWN and then back UP.

        So this is a condition, not a schedule: while the guard is up, ANY
        non-zero offset is corrected in the same frame it appears. It is
        registered here, in the inline script, so it runs before the parallax
        listener that `astro:page-load` adds — the parallax then reads 0 and
        never renders the excursion.

        It gets out of the way immediately. The FIRST real input — touch,
        pointer, wheel, key — releases it, so a reader who flicks the moment
        the page appears is never fought; and it is gone after
        GUARD_MS regardless. The splash lift is exempt while it runs, because
        it legitimately scrolls to a non-zero offset at the end (it is also
        always preceded by an input, which has already released the guard).
      */
      const GUARD_MS = 3000;
      const GESTURES = ['touchstart', 'pointerdown', 'wheel', 'keydown'];

      
      const onStrayScroll = () => {
        if (window.scrollY === 0) return;
        if (document.documentElement.classList.contains('splash-lifting')) return;
        jumpToTop();
      };

      const releaseTopGuard = () => {
        clearTimeout(guardTimer);
        window.removeEventListener('scroll', onStrayScroll);
        GESTURES.forEach((type) => window.removeEventListener(type, releaseTopGuard));
      };

      const guardTimer = setTimeout(releaseTopGuard, GUARD_MS);
      window.addEventListener('scroll', onStrayScroll, { passive: true });
      GESTURES.forEach((type) =>
        window.addEventListener(type, releaseTopGuard, { passive: true, once: true })
      );

      /*
        NO HARD LOCK. This used to add `reload-lock` (position: fixed +
        overflow: hidden on html and body) until the first input. It ate the
        first swipe after a refresh, on a Mac trackpad in Chrome/Brave AND on
        iPhone Safari: a swipe is latched to a scroller when it begins, the
        page was still locked then, and the release came too late for that
        swipe. The hold-the-top guard above keeps every restore timing at the
        top on its own (scripts/e2e-reload-scroll.test.mjs, iPhone and iPad).
      */
    }
</script>
